// Part III — Reference. Declared mode: reference.
page ref_mod_acl {
  sites = [:manual]
  title = "acl module (Windows)"

  chapter_header "acl module (Windows)" {
    kicker       = "Reference · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-20"
    version      = "config-weave 0.1.0"
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
`use acl` reads, compares and sets the owner and the DACL (the access
control list) of Windows files, directories and registry keys, without
starting PowerShell for `Get-Acl` or `Set-Acl`. The module is registered on
every platform, so a playbook compiles and validates identically
everywhere; on Linux and macOS every call returns a runtime error. It does
not handle POSIX ACLs. Guard a call with a step condition on `os.family` or
with `sys::family()` from the [sys module](ref_mod_sys). Every function
returns `Result[…, string]`.

A `check` script calls `diff` and an `apply` script calls `set` with the
same spec. `diff` makes exactly the comparison `set` converges, so `set`
followed by `diff` returns an empty list.
PROSE

  h2 "read"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
acl::read(path: string, opts: Value) -> Result[Value, string]
SRC
  }

  p "Reads the owner and the DACL of `path`. It returns a map with these keys."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Key" | "Value" |
      | "`owner`" | "A map `{sid, name}`, or `Value::Null` when the descriptor has no owner." |
      | "`protected`" | "`true` when inheritance is disabled on the object." |
      | "`entries`" | "A list of entry maps, in the order Windows stores them, inherited entries included." |
  }

  p "Each entry map has these keys."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Key" | "Value" |
      | "`sid`" | "The SID string, such as `S-1-5-32-545`." |
      | "`name`" | "The account name, such as `BUILTIN\\Users` or `Everyone`, or `Value::Null` when the SID does not resolve (an app capability SID, a deleted account)." |
      | "`kind`" | "`allow` or `deny`. An entry of any other ACE type (object or callback ACEs) is `other`, with its ACE type number in an extra `type` key and `sid` and `name` set to `Value::Null`." |
      | "`rights`" | "The right names the mask contains (see Rights below)." |
      | "`mask`" | "The access mask as an int, with generic bits mapped (see Rights below)." |
      | "`inherit`" | "The inherit flags: `object`, `container`, `no_propagate`, `inherit_only`. An empty list means the entry applies to this object only." |
      | "`inherited`" | "`true` when the entry was inherited from the parent." |
  }

  p "A NULL DACL, which grants everyone full access, reads as an empty `entries` list. `diff` reports it as a difference."

  h2 "set"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
acl::set(path: string, spec: Value, opts: Value) -> Result[unit, string]
SRC
  }

  p "Writes `spec` to `path`. The spec is a map with these keys."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Key" | "Value" |
      | "`owner`" | "Optional. A principal. Omitted, the owner is left as it is." |
      | "`protected`" | "Optional. `true` disables inheritance, `false` enables it. Omitted, the current setting is kept." |
      | "`entries`" | "Required. The list of explicit entries. Pass `[]` to remove every explicit entry." |
  }

  p "Each entry is a map with these keys."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Key" | "Value" |
      | "`principal`" | "Required. A SID string or an account name (see Principals below)." |
      | "`kind`" | "Required. `allow` or `deny`." |
      | "`rights`" | "A list of right names for the target." |
      | "`mask`" | "An access mask int, for bits that have no name. When both `rights` and `mask` are given, the entry grants their union. At least one is required." |
      | "`inherit`" | "Optional. A list of inherit flags; omitted or empty, the entry applies to this object only." |
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
`set` replaces every explicit entry on the object with `entries`. It writes
them in the Windows canonical order, deny entries before allow entries,
each group in spec order, and drops exact duplicates. It never writes
inherited entries: while inheritance is enabled, Windows recomputes them
from the parent, and a directory or key passes its own inheritable entries
on to its children.

`protected: true` disables inheritance and drops the inherited entries. It
does not copy them in as explicit entries, unlike the Explorer dialog; list
every entry the object needs. `protected: false` enables inheritance again,
and Windows restores the inherited entries from the parent.

Changing the owner needs SeRestorePrivilege and SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege.
`set` enables both on a thread-local copy of the process token for the
call only, which works when config-weave runs as SYSTEM or as an
administrator. When they cannot be enabled and the owner differs from the
spec, `set` returns an `Err` naming the privileges and changes nothing.

Inherit flags mean something only on a container. Windows strips them from
the entries of a plain file, so an entry with inherit flags on a file is an
`Err` from `set` and `diff`, rather than a change that never converges.
PROSE

  h2 "diff"

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
acl::diff(path: string, spec: Value, opts: Value) -> Result[List[string], string]
SRC
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
Returns the differences between the object's descriptor and `spec`, one
human-readable line each. An empty list means converged. The comparison is
exactly the one `set` makes converge:

- The owner is compared by SID, when the spec gives one.
- The `protected` flag is compared when the spec gives it.
- Explicit entries are compared as a set. Each entry is keyed by its SID,
  its kind, its mask with generic bits mapped, and its inherit flags. Order
  and duplicates do not count. A missing entry and an entry the spec does
  not list are each one line, so a changed right shows as both.
- Inherited entries are ignored, unless the spec has `protected: true` and
  the DACL is already protected; then each inherited entry left over is a
  difference.

`diff` takes the same spec and options as `set` and fails on the same
errors.
PROSE

  h2 "Options"

  p <<'PROSE'
`opts` is `Value::Null` or a map. `target` selects what `path` names:
`"file"` (the default) for a file or directory, or `"registry"` for a
registry key. `view` selects the registry view, as in the
[registry module](ref_mod_registry): `"native"` (the default), `"wow64_32"`
or `"wow64_64"`. `view` is valid only with `target: "registry"`. An unknown
key or value is an `Err`.

A registry path uses the registry module's syntax, `HKLM\Software\Vendor\App`.
A file path is passed to Windows as written. `acl` does not follow a final
symlink or junction: it opens the path with FILE_FLAG_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT,
refuses any reparse point with an `Err`, and reads and writes the
descriptor through that one handle, so the object cannot be swapped between
the check and the write. Name the link's target instead. A missing file or
key is an `Err` naming it.
PROSE

  h2 "Principals"

  p <<'PROSE'
A principal in a spec is a SID string (`S-1-5-32-544`) or an account name:
`BUILTIN\Administrators`, `Administrators`, `NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM`,
`DOMAIN\user`, or `.\user` for an account on this computer. Account names
are resolved with LookupAccountNameW. A principal that does not resolve is
an `Err` naming it. Every comparison is by SID, so a spec may name an
account either way.
PROSE

  h2 "Rights"

  p "Rights are lists of names, because wscript has no bitwise operators. The names are the .NET `FileSystemRights` and `RegistryRights` names in snake_case. A right name that is not valid for the target is an `Err` listing the valid names."

  table {
    rows:
      | "File right" | "Mask" |
      | "`full_control`" | "`0x1F01FF`" |
      | "`modify`" | "`0x0301BF`: `read_and_execute`, `write` and `delete`" |
      | "`read_and_execute`" | "`0x0200A9`: `read` and `execute_file`" |
      | "`read`" | "`0x020089`: `read_data`, `read_extended_attributes`, `read_attributes`, `read_permissions`" |
      | "`write`" | "`0x000116`: `write_data`, `append_data`, `write_extended_attributes`, `write_attributes`" |
      | "`read_data` (alias `list_directory`)" | "`0x000001`" |
      | "`write_data` (alias `create_files`)" | "`0x000002`" |
      | "`append_data` (alias `create_directories`)" | "`0x000004`" |
      | "`read_extended_attributes`" | "`0x000008`" |
      | "`write_extended_attributes`" | "`0x000010`" |
      | "`execute_file` (alias `traverse`)" | "`0x000020`" |
      | "`delete_subdirectories_and_files`" | "`0x000040`" |
      | "`read_attributes`" | "`0x000080`" |
      | "`write_attributes`" | "`0x000100`" |
      | "`delete`" | "`0x010000`" |
      | "`read_permissions`" | "`0x020000`" |
      | "`change_permissions`" | "`0x040000`" |
      | "`take_ownership`" | "`0x080000`" |
      | "`synchronize`" | "`0x100000`" |
  }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Registry right" | "Mask" |
      | "`full_control`" | "`0xF003F`" |
      | "`read_key` (alias `execute_key`)" | "`0x20019`: `query_values`, `enumerate_subkeys`, `notify`, `read_permissions`" |
      | "`write_key`" | "`0x20006`: `set_value`, `create_subkey`, `read_permissions`" |
      | "`query_values`" | "`0x00001`" |
      | "`set_value`" | "`0x00002`" |
      | "`create_subkey`" | "`0x00004`" |
      | "`enumerate_subkeys`" | "`0x00008`" |
      | "`notify`" | "`0x00010`" |
      | "`create_link`" | "`0x00020`" |
      | "`delete`" | "`0x10000`" |
      | "`read_permissions`" | "`0x20000`" |
      | "`change_permissions`" | "`0x40000`" |
      | "`take_ownership`" | "`0x80000`" |
  }

  p <<'PROSE'
`read` turns a mask into names in two passes. First it takes each composite
name, largest first (files: `full_control`, `modify`, `read_and_execute`,
`read`, `write`; registry keys: `full_control`, `read_key`, `write_key`),
when all of its bits are in the mask and it covers at least one bit no
earlier name did. Then it adds the single-bit name of each bit still not
covered, in bit order. So `0x1F01FF` reads as `["full_control"]` and
`0x1200A9` as `["read_and_execute", "synchronize"]`. Aliases are accepted
in a spec but never reported. A bit with no name, such as
ACCESS_SYSTEM_SECURITY, appears only in `mask`.

Windows writes the generic bits (GENERIC_READ, GENERIC_WRITE,
GENERIC_EXECUTE, GENERIC_ALL) into some entries, typically the inherit-only
`CREATOR OWNER` entry. `read` maps them to the rights they grant on the
target before reporting `rights` and `mask`: GENERIC_ALL on a file reads as
`full_control` with mask `0x1F01FF`, where `Get-Acl` shows `268435456`.
`diff` compares mapped masks too, and `set` writes a spec's generic bits
already mapped.

`set` writes the mask the spec gives and adds nothing. Explorer and .NET
add `synchronize` to every file allow entry, and a process needs it to open
a file for synchronous IO, so a file allow entry normally lists
`synchronize` alongside its other rights.
PROSE

  p "A resource whose `path` parameter names the object and whose `acl` parameter holds the spec map checks with `diff` and applies with `set`."

  code rust {
    source = <<'SRC'
use acl
use log
use value

fn target(params: Value) -> Result[string, string] {
    if let Some(v) = params.get("path") {
        if let Some(s) = v.as_string() {
            return Ok(s)
        }
    }
    Err("missing 'path' parameter")
}

fn want(params: Value) -> Result[Value, string] {
    match params.get("acl") {
        Some(v) => Ok(v),
        None => Err("missing 'acl' parameter"),
    }
}

fn check(params: Value) -> Result[CheckResult, string] {
    let changes = acl::diff(target(params)?, want(params)?, Value::Null)?
    for line in changes {
        log::info(line)
    }
    if changes.len() == 0 {
        Ok(CheckResult::AlreadyConfigured)
    } else {
        Ok(CheckResult::NotConfigured)
    }
}

fn apply(params: Value) -> Result[ApplyResult, string] {
    acl::set(target(params)?, want(params)?, Value::Null)?
    Ok(ApplyResult::Success)
}
SRC
  }
}
